The '''Northern Confederation occupation of Guajicutea (1812–1824)''' covers the period from the [[Invasion of Guajicutea]] through the [[Virtuous Revolution]]. Following the 1812 invasion, the [[Northern Confederation]] established a colonial administration under military rule, with the [[Partido Unidad Guajicuteana]] (PUG) serving as the civilian instrument of occupation. The occupation government enacted race codes, miscegenation laws, and antiliberal legislation that reversed the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1803|constitutional reforms]] of the previous decade. Resistance to the occupation coalesced in the [[Guarda Revolutionary]], a guerrilla movement that operated from the island's jungle interior. The conflict between the Guarda Revolutionary and occupation forces produced a sustained period of violence known as the [[Bloody Decade]] (1814–1822), marked by internal rebellions, public executions, and collective punishment. In June 1824, the [[Pastry War]] erupted within the Northern Confederation, forcing the withdrawal of occupation troops from Guajicutea. The resulting power vacuum enabled the Virtuous Revolution, which overthrew the PUG government and restored Guajicutean self-governance.

== Occupation government (1812–1813) ==

The Northern Confederation placed Guajicutea under military rule following the 1812 invasion. [[Capitán-General]] Vittorio Calvi, a Bayaran naval officer, was appointed military governor and established his headquarters at [[Waribá]], the island's primary port and naval base. A garrison of Northern Confederation troops was stationed at Waribá and [[Tabakurí]].

The [[Asamblea Nacional]], dissolved during the invasion, was replaced by an appointed advisory council composed of PUG members and Sartan landowners sympathetic to the occupation. [[Ramón Cifuentes]], the NR exile leader who had orchestrated the coup, was installed as the civilian head of the PUG government. The PUG held no electoral mandate; it had been formed after the invasion to provide a local administrative structure for the occupation authorities.

President [[Mateo Kurí]], arrested during the invasion, was exiled to [[Sarta]] in 1813. The choice of Sarta — Guajicutea's former colonial ruler — was intended as a symbolic humiliation, returning the first Guaji head of state to the colonial power from which the republic had won its independence. Kurí remained in Sarta throughout the occupation.

The occupation government's authority extended fully to the coastal cities and the central valleys. The jungle interior and highland regions remained beyond effective control throughout the occupation period.

== Race codes and legal restructuring (1813) ==

In 1813, the occupation government enacted three bodies of legislation that reversed the reforms of the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1803]] and re-imposed a racial hierarchy.

The '''Códigos de Raza''' (Race Codes) established a formal racial classification system dividing the population into Baliscan, Sartan, Guaji, Bayani, and Kaftian categories. Voting rights were restricted to Baliscan and Sartan citizens, disenfranchising the Guaji, Bayani, and Kaftian populations who had been granted citizenship under the 1803 amendment. Property ownership by non-Baliscan and non-Sartan residents was subject to restrictions, and movement between districtos required official permits for non-Baliscan and non-Sartan residents.

The '''Leyes de Mestizaje''' (Miscegenation Laws) prohibited marriages between Baliscan or Sartan residents and members of other racial categories. Existing interracial marriages were declared invalid. Violations were subject to criminal penalties, including imprisonment and forfeiture of property.

The '''Leyes Antiliberales''' (Antiliberal Laws) banned the [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] as a political organization and restricted the activities of the [[Asociación Nuevo Guaji]]. Freedom of the press was curtailed, with publications required to obtain prior authorisation from the occupation government. Public assemblies of more than ten persons required official permits. The secular education system established under the Kurí government was dismantled, and control of schools was returned to the Church.

The legislation collectively restored the political and social order that had existed before the 1803 reforms, with the addition of Baliscan settlers and administrators to the dominant class.

== Rise of the Guarda Revolutionary (1813–1814) ==

The enactment of the race codes and antiliberal laws in 1813 provoked armed resistance. Former Guajicutean military units that had refused to surrender following the 1812 invasion, along with members of the dissolved PD and Guaji community leaders, retreated to the jungle interior of the island. From the volcanic highlands and dense tropical vegetation of the central valleys, these groups organised into the [[Guarda Revolutionary]], a guerrilla movement dedicated to the overthrow of the occupation government.

The Guarda Revolutionary was led by two figures: [[Tomás Yukurí]], a Guaji former army officer who commanded the movement's military operations, and [[Diego Nasarí]], a Bayani PD organiser who directed its political and underground network. Yukurí's knowledge of the highland terrain and Nasarí's connections to the dissolved PD's parish and community networks provided the movement with both military capacity and civilian support infrastructure.

The Guarda Revolutionary conducted hit-and-run attacks on Northern Confederation supply lines, PUG administrative offices, and occupation infrastructure. The fighters used their knowledge of the island's difficult terrain — the highland slopes, river valleys, and dense vegetation — to evade conventional military operations. The movement's tactics represented an early example of sustained guerrilla warfare in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] region.

The Guarda Revolutionary drew support from rural Guaji communities, who provided food, shelter, and intelligence. The movement's ranks were drawn primarily from the Guaji population, with smaller numbers of Bayani and Kaftian fighters who had been disenfranchised by the race codes.

== The Bloody Decade (1814–1822) ==

The period from 1814 to 1822, known as the [[Bloody Decade]] (Sartan: ''Década Sangrienta''), was characterised by sustained conflict between the Guarda Revolutionary and the occupation forces. The Northern Confederation garrison, supplemented by PUG-aligned militias, conducted counterinsurgency operations in the rural districtos. The conflict produced a cycle of guerrilla attacks and state reprisals that affected the civilian population across the island.

The occupation forces employed collective punishment as a counterinsurgency tactic. Villages suspected of supporting the Guarda Revolutionary were subjected to house burnings, in which dwellings and food stores were destroyed. Captured Guarda Revolutionary fighters were subjected to public beheadings, conducted in town plazas as a deterrent. The executions were attended by occupation soldiers and PUG officials and were intended to demonstrate the consequences of resistance.

The violence was concentrated in the rural districtos with significant Guaji populations, particularly in the central valleys and highland foothills. The coastal cities of Tabakurí, Waribá, and [[Barikú]], under direct occupation control, experienced lower levels of direct violence but were subject to political repression, surveillance, and restrictions on movement.

The Guarda Revolutionary was unable to dislodge the occupation forces from the coastal cities but maintained control over the jungle interior throughout the period. The movement's strength fluctuated with the seasons — operations were more active during the dry months and reduced during the storm season, when movement through the jungle was restricted.

The Bloody Decade produced a lasting national trauma. The combination of military violence, collective punishment, and the destruction of communities created a generation of Guajicuteans who had lived under sustained conflict. The period's memory shaped subsequent Guajicutean political culture and attitudes toward foreign intervention.

== Economic conditions under occupation ==

The occupation disrupted Guajicutea's economy. Trade routes that had been established during the republican period were redirected to serve Northern Confederation commercial interests, particularly those of [[Bayara]]. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]], the island's principal industrial installation, was placed under Northern Confederation military control and repurposed for naval maintenance and repair.

Taxation was increased to support the occupation garrison. Agricultural production in the rural districtos was disrupted by the conflict, as fields were abandoned by farmers fleeing violence or conscripted by the Guarda Revolutionary. Food shortages occurred in the coastal cities, which depended on rural agricultural production.

The pre-occupation manufacturing sector, which had developed during the republican period as the economy shifted from plantation agriculture to light manufacturing, declined under occupation. Northern Confederation commercial interests favoured the export of raw materials over local manufacturing.

== The Pastry War and troop withdrawal (1824) ==

In June 1824, the [[Pastry War]] (Baliscano: ''Guerra de Pastisceri'') erupted within the Northern Confederation. The conflict, fought between the republics of [[Bayara]] and [[Hesperia]], arose from intercommunal tensions in the Estuarian wetlands and disputes over the extent of confederal power. The war required the Northern Confederation to redeploy military forces from external commitments to internal theatres.

The Pastry War prompted the withdrawal of Hesperian troops from the Guajicutea occupation garrison. The remaining Bayaran garrison was reduced below the level necessary to maintain control of the island. Capitán-General Marco Vendrell, who had succeeded Calvi as military governor in 1820, oversaw the withdrawal.

The reduction of occupation forces created a power vacuum. The PUG government, dependent on Northern Confederation military support, was unable to maintain authority over the island without the garrison.

== The Virtuous Revolution (1824) ==

The [[Virtuous Revolution]] (Sartan: ''Revolución Virtuosa'') was the overthrow of the PUG occupation government on 12 July 1824, following the withdrawal of Northern Confederation troops. The Guarda Revolutionary, under the military command of Tomás Yukurí, emerged from the jungle interior and moved toward the coastal cities. Simultaneously, popular uprisings occurred in Tabakurí and Waribá, organised by underground PD networks coordinated by Diego Nasarí.

The PUG government collapsed without military support. Ramón Cifuentes fled Guajicutea. The Northern Confederation, consumed by the Pastry War and its aftermath, was unable to respond. The revolution was accomplished with minimal bloodshed, as the remaining occupation forces withdrew rather than engage the revolutionary movement.

The revolution was termed "virtuous" to distinguish it from the violent occupation period and to assert its legitimacy as the restoration of Guajicutean sovereignty. The name emphasised the moral authority of the resistance over the illegitimate occupation government.

== Aftermath ==

The Virtuous Revolution restored Guajicutean self-governance. The race codes, miscegenation laws, and antiliberal legislation were repealed. The constitutional framework of the 1803 amendment was restored, including universal citizenship and the elected Asamblea Nacional.

The Northern Confederation did not formally acknowledge the loss of Guajicutea. The Confederation's internal crisis, which would culminate in the [[Compromise of 1827]] and the subsequent collapse of the Confederation in 1828, prevented any response to the revolution.

The legacy of the occupation period and the Bloody Decade shaped Guajicutean political culture for subsequent generations. The Guarda Revolutionary, which transitioned from a guerrilla movement to a political organisation, became a significant force in post-revolution politics.

